# Decision on Media Loading and Snapping

## Two Records under the Same Entry Point

The first version put `Nested loader completion and generator contract` and `Reversible media snapping` together because troubleshooting media issues often starts from the same object. After review, this entry point remains, but it is no longer taken to mean that the two requirements share a point of responsibility.

The local entry point for loading is still `src/inflators/media-loader.ts`. What is directly visible is:

```ts
import { addComponent } from "bitecs";
import { HubsWorld } from "../app";
import { MediaLoader } from "../bit-components";
import { MEDIA_LOADER_FLAGS } from "../bit-systems/media-loading";
```

`addComponent` shows that this code is involved in writing components. `HubsWorld` shows that the logic operates with world context. `MediaLoader` and `MEDIA_LOADER_FLAGS` let us continue tracing the loader object and load marker from here. The four imports do not establish a call sequence or indicate where snap rollback is executed.

The review compared documentation boundaries, not complete implementation proposals.

**Option A: One overall media result.** Collapse the appearance of the root object, descendant readiness, media loading, and position rollback into a single state. This keeps the page short and makes it easy for later discussions to call it "media complete." The problem is that when this state does not hold, there is no way to tell whether nested objects have not been processed yet, the loader object is still present, the media association was not retained, or the position has only changed locally.

**Option B: Two issue tracks.** The loading track records media object preparation, nesting relationships, and completion checks; the snapping track records position changes and the scope of restoration. Both use the media object as the starting point for lookups, but they do not share a single overall completion result. The cost is that every record must specify whether it is discussing loading or snapping.

This revision confirms Option B. It governs documentation only: a loading issue cannot be closed just because we can "see the root object"; a snapping issue cannot be closed just because "loading has finished."

## Earlier Wording Retained

The first draft had a convenient rule of thumb:

> Once the root object is visible, we can treat the loader as complete; snap undo can wait until the next position update.

This passage remains where it was. It explains why the two names initially appeared on the same page: at the time, the root object on screen, the loader object, and position changes were all loosely called "media state." But it is no longer a basis for decisions.

The root object being visible is only an observation on screen; it cannot substitute for a readiness check on nested descendants. A position that appears to return to its original location also does not automatically mean that media references or collision shapes have been restored at the same time. For similar records in the future, first state clearly whether the actual observation concerns position, references, or shapes; do not use "snap back" to cover three different things.

Another early idea was to treat the loader as an object for the root node only, leaving external callers to wait for child nodes to be ready. That idea named neither the caller nor the wait condition, so it is no longer a working conclusion for this page. It remains on record to remind future reviews not to treat an unnamed caller as an established boundary.

## Findings Retained from This Review

`MediaLoader` is the explicitly named object on the loading track, and `MEDIA_LOADER_FLAGS` is an explicitly identified neighbor on the loading-system side. Both appear in `media-loader.ts`, so that file is a starting point for continuing to trace declarations and references on the loading side.

The snapping track has only the requirement name `Reversible media snapping` and the scope "reversible position changes." Until corresponding references are seen, do not specify concrete rollback actions or describe the loading file as the responsible entry point for snapping.

Following review, this page uses the following sequence:

1. First distinguish whether the investigation is about loading or snapping;
2. For loading, continue tracing from `MediaLoader`, the load marker, and the world context;
3. For snapping, first check position, media references, and collision shapes separately;
4. Record a shared relationship only when the same source material connects these objects.

## Still Unresolved

- Which actual reference determines the completion state of nested media descendants;
- Whether there is a distinct boundary between the root object becoming visible and loading finishing;
- Whether undoing a snap reads or rewrites media references;
- Whether the collision shape and the restoration of the position come from the same action;
- Whether the snapping side continues processing the same object after the loading side has finished.

These questions do not change Option B. They are exactly why Option B is being kept: until actual references establish the relationships between the objects, do not substitute a single "media complete" state for all the unknowns.
